/// Chapter 35 of the guide, written from md-docs/recipes/environments.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page EnvironmentsGuide(path: "/docs/guide/environments", title: "Environments", description: "Set env values from the config, a .env file or the shell; learn which wins; keep private names out of the bundle; and build once per environment.", layout: DocsShell("Environments", chapter: t("nav.group.ship"), blurb: "Set values that change per build (an API address, a feature switch, a public key) from the config, a .env file or the shell, and choose which a page may read.", source: "md-docs/recipes/environments.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "read-a-value-in-a-page", label: "Read a value in a page"), Anchor(id: "set-a-value", label: "Set a value"), Anchor(id: "keep-a-private-value-out-of-the-bundle", label: "Keep a private value out of the bundle"), Anchor(id: "name-your-own-values-something-other-than-env", label: "Name your own values something other than env"), Anchor(id: "build-once-per-environment", label: "Build once per environment"), Anchor(id: "change-other-settings-per-deploy", label: "Change other settings per deploy"), Anchor(id: "audit-what-the-project-reads", label: "Audit what the project reads")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/deploying", prevLabel: t("ch.34"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/desktop", nextLabel: t("ch.36"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to give a site values that differ between your machine, staging and production: the API's address, an analytics key, a feature switch. A page reads each one as `env.NAME`. The build fixes the value **at build time**: it writes the value into the output, so there is nothing to configure on the host.\n\nIn this chapter:\n\n- Set a value in the config, a `.env` file or the shell.\n- Choose which names a page may read, and keep the rest out of the bundle.\n- Build the same source once per environment.")

        Heading("Read a value in a page", id: "read-a-value-in-a-page").h2

        Markdown("To read a value, write `env.NAME`. This page reads the API's address and a feature switch, and falls back to defaults when nothing sets them:")

        CodeBlock(#"const API = env.PUBLIC_API ?? "/api"
const FEATURES = { billing: env.PUBLIC_BILLING == "on" }

page Rows(path: "/", title: "Rows", description: "Rows from the API.") {
    resource rows = fetch("{API}/rows")
    Heading("Rows").h1
    if FEATURES.billing { Link("Billing", to: "/billing") }
    match rows {
        loading { Spinner }
        error(e) { Text(e.message).danger }
        ready(r) { Text("{r.length} rows") }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("A name that nothing sets reads as nothing (`null`), so `??` supplies the default. Keep the values in `const`s at the top of the file, as above, so every page reads them from one place.")

        Heading("Set a value", id: "set-a-value").h2

        Markdown("You can set a value in three places. When two set the same name, the later one in this list wins:\n\n1. **The config's `env` map**, committed with the project:\n\n   ```json \{ \"env\": \{ \"PUBLIC_API\": \"/api\", \"PUBLIC_BILLING\": \"off\" \} \} ```\n\n2. **A `.env` file** beside `webfluent.app.json`, which you do not commit. `wf init` adds `.env` to `.gitignore`. Each line is `NAME=value`. A line that starts with `#` is a comment, a line may start with `export `, and a value may be in single or double quotes:\n\n   ```bash PUBLIC_API=http://localhost:8080/api PUBLIC_BILLING=on ```\n\n3. **The shell** that runs the build, for CI and one-off builds:\n\n   ```bash PUBLIC_API=https://api.example.com wf build ```\n\n   The shell supplies a name that begins `PUBLIC_`, or a private name the config or `.env` already declares. It does not bring in every variable the build happens to run with, such as `HOME`.\n\n   A name that `public_env` makes public without the prefix takes its value from the config or `.env` only — `public_env` is part of the project, and a project you cloned could list `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` there to publish your shell's value. To let the shell supply one, name it in `WF_ALLOW_SHELL_ENV`, comma-separated:\n\n   ```bash WF_ALLOW_SHELL_ENV=ANALYTICS_ID ANALYTICS_ID=UA-2 wf build ```")

        Callout("Versions before 4.1 read only the config's `env` map.", kind: .note)

        Heading("Keep a private value out of the bundle", id: "keep-a-private-value-out-of-the-bundle").h2

        Markdown("The build replaces `env.NAME` with its value in the JavaScript and HTML that every reader downloads. That is right for an API address and wrong for an API key. So a page, a component, a store or an `api` block may read only a name that says it is public:\n\n- a name that begins with **`PUBLIC_`**, or\n- a name listed in the config's **`public_env`**.\n\nIn this config, `env.PUBLIC_API` and `env.ANALYTICS_ID` are public, and `env.STRIPE_SECRET` is private:")

        CodeBlock(#"{
  "env": { "PUBLIC_API": "/api/v1", "ANALYTICS_ID": "UA-1", "STRIPE_SECRET": "sk_…" },
  "public_env": ["ANALYTICS_ID"]
}"#, title: "json")

        Markdown("A page may read `env.PUBLIC_API` and `env.ANALYTICS_ID`. Reading `env.STRIPE_SECRET` from a page is a compile error ([`E108`](/docs/guide/diagnostics#e108-an-env-name-that-is-not-public)) that names the file and the line.\n\nA private value is still available to [server rendering](/docs/guide/server-rendering), which runs on a server, where a secret stays secret. It never reaches the bundle: the build writes only public values into it.")

        Callout("Adding a name to `public_env` publishes its value to every reader of the site, and every build says so with a warning naming it. A secret that a third party needs belongs in a request your server makes, not in a page ([Security](/docs/guide/security#keep-secrets-out-of-the-bundle)).", kind: .caution)

        Heading("Name your own values something other than env", id: "name-your-own-values-something-other-than-env").h2

        Markdown("`env` is the language's name for these settings, and nothing else may use it. A `state`, a prop, a route parameter, a loop item or a top-level `const` called `env` is an error ([`E118`](/docs/guide/diagnostics#e118-a-name-the-language-already-has)). For a value that says which stage a page is about, use a name such as `target` or `stage`.")

        Heading("Build once per environment", id: "build-once-per-environment").h2

        Markdown("To deploy the same source to several environments, run one build per environment, each with its own values:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build                                              # .env on your machine"),
                Line(command: "PUBLIC_API=https://staging.example.com/api wf build   # staging"),
                Line(command: "PUBLIC_API=https://api.example.com wf build           # production")
            ])

        Markdown("In CI, set the variables on the job's build step:")

        CodeBlock(#"- run: ~/.webfluent/bin/wf build
  env:
    PUBLIC_API: https://api.example.com
    PUBLIC_ANALYTICS_ID: ${{ vars.ANALYTICS_ID }}"#, title: "yaml")

        Markdown("Each build writes its own `build/` folder. Deploy each one to its own host ([Deploying](/docs/guide/deploying)).")

        Heading("Change other settings per deploy", id: "change-other-settings-per-deploy").h2

        Markdown("A few settings outside `env` also change between deploys:")

        Table(caption: "Change other settings per deploy") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Setting").header
                    Table.Cell("What it is").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`build.base_path`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The sub-path the site is served under. The build prefixes every link, asset and route with it ([Deploying](/docs/guide/deploying))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`meta.site_url`") }
                    Table.Cell("The site's absolute address, for canonical links, the sitemap and sharing tags")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`theme.tokens`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Design tokens a pipeline supplies, on top of the theme: `\{ \"theme\": \{ \"tokens\": \{ \"color-primary\": \"#8B5CF6\" \} \} \}`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("These settings live in `webfluent.app.json`. There is no separate config file per environment. A pipeline that needs different settings writes the file before it builds; `wf config set` edits it from a script:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf config set build.base_path /staging")
            ])

        Heading("Audit what the project reads", id: "audit-what-the-project-reads").h2

        Markdown("To list every `env` name the project has, run `wf audit`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf audit")
            ])

        Markdown("It prints each name, where it is read, and whether it is public, beside everything else the site trusts ([Security](/docs/guide/security#audit-the-project)). Run it before a release, and when you review a change that adds a name.")
    }
}
